1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:06,770
Your horned helmet does not exist. The horns were invented in eighteen seventy-six, for an opera.

2
00:00:07,290 --> 00:00:14,457
Only one nearly complete Viking helmet has ever been dug up. It's a plain iron cap. No horns.

3
00:00:14,497 --> 00:00:21,814
A man dug it out of a grave in Norway in nineteen forty-three. In pieces. It took years to put back together.

4
00:00:22,134 --> 00:00:28,869
It's dawn. You're a farmer in Norway, around the year nine ninety. And today, you're going on a raid.

5
00:00:28,909 --> 00:00:33,603
But first, you have to feed the pigs. Nobody puts that in the sagas.

6
00:00:35,003 --> 00:00:37,940
You wake up in one long room, full of smoke.

7
00:00:37,980 --> 00:00:45,561
There's no chimney. The fire burns in the middle of the floor, and the smoke leaves through a hole in the roof when it feels like it.

8
00:00:45,601 --> 00:00:54,755
The walls are turf. Blocks of grass, stacked on stones. In Iceland there are barely any trees, so your house is basically a hill with a door.

9
00:00:54,795 --> 00:01:00,536
Your eyes sting. Your hair smells like a campfire. Your wife hands you something.

10
00:01:02,336 --> 00:01:03,736
A comb.

11
00:01:04,091 --> 00:01:11,756
Bad news for the legend. Viking graves are full of combs, tweezers, and little ear scoops. For men and for women.

12
00:01:11,796 --> 00:01:16,438
Saturday, in Old Norse, is laugardagr. It means washing day.

13
00:01:16,478 --> 00:01:23,845
One English chronicler, writing later, complained that Danish men combed their hair so often, they stole English women.

14
00:01:23,885 --> 00:01:28,707
So you are clean. Cleaner than the monks you're about to rob. Sorry.

15
00:01:30,607 --> 00:01:36,115
Before you leave, one problem. Your neighbour owes you a cow. He says he doesn't.

16
00:01:36,155 --> 00:01:43,214
In Iceland there's no king and no great lords. So there's no one to arrest him. Instead, there's the Thing.

17
00:01:43,254 --> 00:01:53,312
The island is brand new. Settlers arrive mostly between eight seventy and nine thirty. The Book of Settlements names four hundred and thirty-five of them.

18
00:01:53,352 --> 00:02:03,527
Around nine thirty, the Icelanders started their Althing. Once a year, free men gather on a plain. A lawspeaker recites the law from a rock. From memory.

19
00:02:03,567 --> 00:02:10,501
You win the case. Great. Nobody collects the cow for you. In the sagas, that's mostly your problem.

20
00:02:10,921 --> 00:02:20,035
And killing a man? That's settled with a payment. The old word is mangeld. Man-payment. The sagas are full of arguments over the price.

21
00:02:20,075 --> 00:02:30,084
The big vote comes in the year one thousand. According to Njal's saga, the Althing chooses Christianity. The old gods lose, on a technicality.

22
00:02:31,484 --> 00:02:35,744
Who shovels the manure on your farm? Not you. A thrall.

23
00:02:35,784 --> 00:02:44,337
Thralls were slaves. Historians argue about how many. Estimates run from a few percent of the people, up to one in four in Norway.

24
00:02:44,377 --> 00:02:50,870
They came from raids, from slave markets, or from debt. The child of two thralls was born a thrall.

25
00:02:50,910 --> 00:02:54,659
Some saved enough to buy their freedom. Most did not.

26
00:02:54,699 --> 00:03:01,356
And that's part of why you're going on a raid. More silver. More thralls. Don't think about it too hard.

27
00:03:03,156 --> 00:03:12,715
Noon. Your crew is at the beach. A big longship, like the Sea Stallion of Glendalough, is twenty-nine meters long and carries sixty to seventy people.

28
00:03:12,755 --> 00:03:18,234
Under sail, it averages six to eight knots. Under oars, about two and a half.

29
00:03:18,274 --> 00:03:26,742
Sixty people on one open boat. Nobody gets a cabin. Nobody gets a toilet. Everybody gets an opinion.

30
00:03:26,782 --> 00:03:33,647
So when the wind dies, you row. For days. Your hands will blister. Your back will file a complaint.

31
00:03:33,687 --> 00:03:39,925
At least your sword is good. If it says Ulfberht on the blade, that's the best brand money can buy.

32
00:03:39,965 --> 00:03:48,529
About one hundred and sixty-seven are known. Many are spelled wrong. So some of them are fakes. Even Vikings bought knock-offs.

33
00:03:50,329 --> 00:03:57,042
Day nine. You land at a small island monastery. This is Lindisfarne, June seven ninety-three.

34
00:03:57,082 --> 00:04:02,096
You think you're the hero of this story. The monks think you're the end of the world.

35
00:04:02,616 --> 00:04:08,521
The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle says heathen men ravaged God's church with looting and slaughter.

36
00:04:08,561 --> 00:04:15,206
A scholar named Alcuin wrote from Charlemagne's court to the bishop, shocked that such a thing was even possible.

37
00:04:15,246 --> 00:04:20,820
That one raid starts three centuries of the word Viking meaning one thing. Terror.

38
00:04:22,820 --> 00:04:26,649
Years later, England decides to pay you to stay away.

39
00:04:26,689 --> 00:04:35,964
Nine ninety-one: ten thousand pounds. Nine ninety-four: sixteen thousand. Then twenty-four. Then thirty. Then forty-eight.

40
00:04:36,004 --> 00:04:42,718
In 1018, England pays seventy-two thousand pounds. Plus ten and a half thousand from London alone.

41
00:04:42,758 --> 00:04:48,562
It's called Danegeld. Which is not what anyone called it back then. It was just tribute.

42
00:04:48,602 --> 00:04:56,087
After ten twelve, it becomes a yearly tax, to pay the fleet. England is literally funding the people who attack it.

43
00:04:56,127 --> 00:05:00,820
It works. Until you come back, with a bigger fleet, and a higher price.

44
00:05:02,720 --> 00:05:05,366
Now, the bad news. About you.

45
00:05:05,886 --> 00:05:16,540
In York, archaeologists dug up Viking latrine pits full of whipworm eggs. Roundworm too. There's a famous fossilized poop on display, with hundreds of eggs in it.

46
00:05:16,580 --> 00:05:24,806
It was found in nineteen seventy-two, under a bank in York. They keep it in a museum. It is the most famous poop in history.

47
00:05:24,846 --> 00:05:30,071
Some men in Sweden filed grooves into their front teeth. Nobody knows why.

48
00:05:30,111 --> 00:05:35,170
In Denmark, the adults buried at Ribe died at around thirty-eight, on average.

49
00:05:35,210 --> 00:05:43,163
And for women, childbirth was the biggest danger. Researchers say deaths were very high, in every pre-industrial society.

50
00:05:44,563 --> 00:05:48,907
But here's a surprise. Back home, your wife is running the show.

51
00:05:48,947 --> 00:05:55,870
In five hundred and eleven graves on Gotland, thirty-four women were buried with keys. Only seven men.

52
00:05:55,910 --> 00:06:03,195
And in Iceland's laws, a wife could divorce you. If it was your fault, she could take back everything her family brought.

53
00:06:03,235 --> 00:06:06,940
So don't be a bad husband. Your wife has the keys.

54
00:06:08,840 --> 00:06:13,246
If you die fighting, you go to Valhalla. That's what the poems say.

55
00:06:13,286 --> 00:06:18,565
It has five hundred and forty doors. Eight hundred warriors walk through each one.

56
00:06:18,605 --> 00:06:24,231
That's four hundred and thirty-two thousand people, in a hall, waiting for the end of the world.

57
00:06:24,651 --> 00:06:31,934
Those poems were written down by Christians, two centuries later. So nobody knows what your neighbours truly believed.

58
00:06:33,334 --> 00:06:38,782
Maybe you sail west. Erik the Red settles Greenland around nine eighty-five.

59
00:06:38,822 --> 00:06:48,458
And at L'Anse aux Meadows in Canada, wood was cut in the year ten twenty-one. Dated by a solar storm in nine ninety-three, recorded in the tree rings.

60
00:06:48,498 --> 00:06:53,694
So yes. Vikings reached America, five hundred years before Columbus.

61
00:06:54,114 --> 00:07:01,568
Remember the helmet. No horns. Would you have survived the smoke, the sea, or the Thing? Tell me in the comments.
